.TH RUSSELL 1 "October 2026" "Russell 3.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
russell \- compiler driver for the Russell programming language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rc
.RI [ options ] " file.r " [ object-file ...]
.br
.B rhelp
.RI [ \-k ] " [topic]"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rc
compiles a Russell source file and, unless instructed otherwise, links the
resulting program.  The default output file is
.IR a.out .
The Linux port produces 32-bit i386 ELF executables and uses the system
Boehm garbage collector.
.PP
The compiler normally finds its installed compiler passes, runtime object,
and standard environment automatically.  They are installed below the
architecture-dependent Russell data directory.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c
Compile only.  Produce an object file and a Russell interface file, but do
not link an executable.
.TP
.B \-G
Generate Russell Intermediate Code in a
.I .G
file only.
.TP
.B \-O
Enable the historical local optimizer.  Repeating this option enables
additional historical optimization passes.
.TP
.B \-o \fIname\fR
Write the linked program to
.IR name .
.TP
.B \-p
Run the C preprocessor on the Russell source before compilation.
.TP
.B \-S
Generate assembly code but do not assemble or link it.
.TP
.B \-r
Leave the output relocatable for a later link step.
.SH HELP
.B rhelp
prints the on-line Russell manual.  With a topic, it prints help for that
topic; for example:
.PP
.EX
rhelp compiling
rhelp inference
.EE
.PP
Use
.B rhelp -k
to search the manual by keyword.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/lib/russell/
Compiler passes, runtime objects, standard environment, and on-line help
files on a 32-bit Fedora installation.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/russell/
Examples and additional Russell documentation.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RUSSELL_ROOT
Overrides the compiler data directory.  This is normally set by the
installed
.B rc
wrapper and is useful when testing a staged installation.
.TP
.B RUSSELL_BASE
Overrides the base directory used by
.BR rhelp (1).
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
rc -o fact /usr/share/doc/russell/examples/fact.r
\&./fact
.EE
.SH AUTHORS
Russell was developed by Alan Demers, J. Donahue, Hans-Juergen Boehm and
others.  The Linux/i386 port is documented in the installed PORTING files.
